  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research serial stencil efficiency: replace separately allocated rows with contiguous storage, eliminate the interior copy pass by swapping two buffers, and make the update/error loop amenable to ARM64 SIMD. Initialize both buffers’ boundaries identically to the original grid, including tiny grids whose boundaries contain heat. Overwrite every required interior cell before reading it. Try a branchless logical-OR convergence reduction and appropriate alignment without reassociating stencil additions. Measure layout, swapping and vectorization changes separately where the existing budget permits; retain the fastest correct standalone program.",
        "B": "Research four-core execution using parallel facilities supported by the unchanged build; do not assume OpenMP is enabled. Prefer a persistent worker team, static contiguous row ownership, thread-local convergence flags, and synchronized global stopping and buffer swaps. Every iteration must read only the previous grid, complete all writes, combine flags, and publish the resulting buffer before continuing. Compare serial, two-worker and four-worker variants end to end. Investigate a general work-size threshold for avoiding parallel overhead on small inputs, not dispatch based on test identities. Keep stdout serial and deterministic.",
        "C": "Research output overhead independently of stencil changes. Compare existing fprintf calls with bounded row/block buffering and larger writes, preserving exactly six fractional digits, a trailing space after every value, and each row’s newline. If formatting remains expensive, investigate an exact binary64-to-fixed-six-decimal converter: all computed temperatures remain within [0,100], but rounding must match libc, including ties, carries, tiny values and zero. Derive rounding from the binary significand/exponent rather than assuming multiplication by 1e6 followed by rounding is equivalent. Retain libc formatting unless equivalence is established. Measure total runtime, not merely formatting throughput.",
        "G": "Integrate validated programs produced by A/B/C, starting from the fastest measured correct standalone candidate. Evaluate promising pairwise combinations before a full combination; shared layouts, parallel synchronization, SIMD and output buffering can interact negatively. Use the unchanged correctness checks and timing protocol within the existing budget, and retain the baseline and standalone winners as rollback candidates. Audit tiny grids, zero iterations, odd/even dimensions, iteration-limit termination, convergence termination, final-buffer selection and exact formatting. Select by measured end-to-end performance, never summed component speedups. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions or budgets.",
        "analysis": "Only Problem E and src/laplace_seq.c are relevant. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 66.12 ms, with samples spanning 65.58–66.55 ms. No workload dimensions or phase measurements are supplied, so neither stencil work nor output is established as dominant. The visible opportunities are the full-grid copy, scalar convergence handling, unused CPU cores and per-value formatting. Target ARM64 with four cores and no GPU. Preserve actual source semantics: synchronous double-precision Jacobi updates, the original addition order, fixed initialized boundaries, strict error > (double)1.0e-5f, and stopping after the first iteration with no exceeding cell or at the iteration limit. The converging iteration’s result must be printed; nonpositive iteration limits retain the initial grid. No speedup has yet been demonstrated."
      },
      "request_sha256": "958129573eecdfc43c61e29b0e909d3eb4709fbb458ed03afecc63c6ee659aff",
